hare, so acceleration there could not offset added overhead (data movement/reformatting, type conversions, launch/scheduling effects, or reduced occupancy). NCU after the change still indicates compute-dominated behavior, but performance worsened, meaning compute pressure was not reduced in a useful way. This suggests either misattribution of hotspot contribution (softmax/scalar reduction still dominant) or an inefficient tensor-core implementation for this problem shape.\\n**Lessons**:\\n  - Do hotspot attribution before rewriting: confirm percentage of time in QK/PV matmul vs softmax/reduction/scalar ops.\\n  - A compute-bound label alone is insufficient; optimize the dominant compute instructions, not just any compute region.\\n  - Tensor-core/MFMA paths can regress when shapes, tiling, or conversion overheads are unfavorable.\\n  - Track secondary effects (occupancy, register pressure, instruction mix, conversion ops) when introducing mixed-precision paths.\\n\\n#### Round 1 Reflection [Ineffective]\\n**Diagnosis**: Attention math density (QK matmul + softmax + PV matmul) dominates runtime relative to bytes moved.\\n**Fix Applied**: Reduce FLOPs per output by using lower-precision/faster math paths where acceptable (e.g., relaxed softmax precision or approximate attention variants) and retune BLOCK_N/BLOCK_M for fewer total dot/exp operations.\\n**Expected**: If compute was the true bottleneck, reducing FLOPs via lower-precision/approximate math and better block sizing should have reduced kernel time and possibly lowered compute SOL pressure.\\n**Actual**: Kernel time regressed significantly from 0.3863ms to 0.7827ms (-102.6% improvement), despite passing verification. (-102.6%)\\n**Analysis**: The diagnosis appears directionally correct because NCU SOL shows high compute utilization (90.3%) and low memory pressure (17.6%), consistent with a compute-bound kernel. However, the applied changes did not translate into speedup. This suggests the new math path and/or retuned BLOCK_N/BLOCK_M increased effective cost (e.g., worse tensor-core usage, reduced occupancy, higher instruction overhead, poorer tiling efficiency, or slower softmax implementation despite nominally fewer FLOPs). Since config changes were listed as none, the intended retuning may not have been realized in a beneficial way at runtime. Net result: compute bottleneck remained, and throughput got worse.\\n**Lessons**:\\n  - A correct bottleneck diagnosis is not sufficient; implementation details (kernel mapping, tensor-core alignment, occupancy) determine whether theoretical FLOP reductions produce real speedups.\\n  - Approximate/lower-precision paths must be validated for hardware efficiency, not just arithmetic count, because they can introduce extra conversions or non-fused instructions.\\n  - Track whether tuning knobs actually changed effective launch/config behavior; intended retuning with no concrete config delta is a red flag.\\n\\n\\nGuidance for this round:\\n- AVOID: Spending early optimization rounds on memory-layout micro-tuning when profiling indicates a compute-path mismatch.; Relying on generic vector ALU paths for BF16 GEMM-like attention operations.; Applying approximate/sparse attention transformations without proving net kernel-level savings for the target shapes.; Changing multiple tuning knobs at once when introducing algorithmic changes, which makes regressions hard to attribute.; Applying large architectural rewrites (tensor-core conversion) without first validating that the targeted subgraph is the top-time contributor.; Assuming high compute SOL automatically means matmul is the right optimization target.; Applying approximate math changes and tile retuning together in one step without isolating their individual impact.; Assuming fewer FLOPs automatically means faster runtime in attention kernels.; Making optimization claims without confirming concrete kernel config/occupancy/tensor-core path changes.- PRIORITIZE: Prioritize kernel variants that guarantee tensor/matrix-core lowering for dominant matmul regions.; Validate each round with SOL split (compute vs memory) to ensure optimization effort matches the true bottleneck.; Continue with compute-side refinements next (tile sizes, pipeline stages, occupancy/register balance) now that matrix cores are engaged.; Profile instruction mix and tensor-core utilization (e.g., HMMA issue rate, eligible warps, occupancy) before/after to confirm real compute savings.; Run controlled ablations: keep algorithm fixed while sweeping BLOCK_M/N/D, num_warps, num_stages; then apply approximation separately.; Prefer fused exact kernels (FlashAttention-style IO-aware tiling) or better matmul tiling/pipeline tuning first, since memory SOL is low and compute efficiency is the limiter.; Test shape-specific sparse/low-rank thresholds and prune patterns only where they improve hardware utilization and reduce overhead.; Profile-driven decomposition: quantify time and instruction share for softmax exp/reduction vs matmul before choosing fixes.; Optimize softmax/reduction path (fused reductions, warp-level primitives, numerically safe but cheaper FP32 scope, fewer passes over data).; Test shape-aware MFMA tiling and minimize BF16<->FP32 conversion/reformat overhead before full rollout.; Use incremental A/B microbenchmarks per subkernel (QK, softmax, PV) to verify each change improves end-to-end time.; Run ablations: test precision-path change alone, then block-size change alone, to identify which component caused regression.; Prioritize tile shapes that maximize tensor-core utilization and occupancy for QK and PV GEMMs; verify with instruction mix and achieved occupancy metrics.; Use fused/online softmax and minimize type conversions and exp/log overhead; profile instruction-level hotspots before further algorithmic approximations.; Add guardrail metrics per round (achieved TFLOPs, tensor-core active, occupancy, register spill) to ensure compute optimizations improve actual execution efficiency.\\n\\n## PERFORMANCE TARGET\\n- PyTorch Eager baseline: 0.2918 ms\\n- Current best kernel: 0.3863 ms\\n- Target: Improve by at least 10% (< 0.3477 ms)\\n- Maintain numerical correctness (atol=1e-4 or rtol=1e-4)\\n- Preserve public API (same inputs/outputs, shapes, dtypes)\\n\\n## REQUIREMENTS\\n1. Apply the recommended fixes above to address the COMPUTE bottleneck\\n2. The implementation must be a complete, valid Python file\\n3. Main function must be named 'kernel_function' wrapping the Triton kernel\\n4. Keep the wrapper free of PyTorch compute primitives\\n\\n## OUTPUT FORMAT\\nOutput complete optimized kernel code in ```python blocks.\\nInclude only: imports, Triton kernel (@triton.jit), wrapper function (kernel_function).\\nNo testing code, benchmarks, or explanatory comments.\\n\\nGenerate the complete optimized kernel implementation:\"}]}],\"max_output_tokens\":24576,\"model\":\"gpt-5.3-codex\"}"}}, "request_meta": {"model": "gpt-5.3-codex", "max_output_tokens": 24576}, "response": {"http_version": "HTTP/1.1", "headers": [["Date", "Mon, 23 Mar 2026 03:35:15 GMT"], ["Content-Type", "application/json"], ["Transfer-Encoding", "chunked"], ["Connection", "keep-alive"], ["Server", "cloudflare"], ["x-ratelimit-limit-requests", "15000"], ["x-ratelimit-limit-tokens", "40000000"], ["x-ratelimit-remaining-requests", "14999"], ["x-ratelimit-remaining-tokens", "39998782"], ["x-ratelimit-reset-requests", "4ms"], ["x-ratelimit-reset-tokens", "1ms"], ["openai-version", "2020-10-01"], ["openai-organization", "user-1cxdgrbvyzsglmmbqabhwk1k"], ["openai-project", "proj_XNNf74ZO7x4HoDwE8wx7GRzr"], ["x-request-id", "req_aec44ea54843430ab9912157a1f989c1"], ["openai-processing-ms", "16067"], ["cf-cache-status", "DYNAMIC"], ["X-Content-Type-Options", "nosniff"], ["Strict-Transport-Security", "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload"], ["Content-Encoding", "gzip"], ["CF-RAY", "9e0a622a5cb857eb-DFW"], ["alt-svc", "h3=\":443\"; ma=86400"]], "content_length_head